<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-eigenvalues" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors (Spectral Theory)</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Most vectors change direction when a matrix acts on them. The exceptions <mdash />
      the eigenvectors <mdash /> are merely stretched, and if there are enough of them
      they form a basis in which the matrix becomes diagonal. That change of viewpoint
      turns hard questions, such as computing a large power of a matrix or the long-run
      behaviour of a process, into easy ones.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <xi:include href="eigenvalues/sec-eigenvalues.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="eigenvalues/sec-diagonalization.ptx" />
</chapter>
